this is the sound of a new generation of  ukrainians unbowed by war resurrecting  what's been lost  each weekend hundreds of young  volunteers from across ukraine and as  far away as london donate their own  money raise more through crowdfunding  and hire coaches to carry them two hours  north from kiev to get here  then they go to work  they call it a cleanup rave we cannot  all be on the front line  shooting  and struggling for our country but we  can do something useful here staying at  our homes at our country  so someone destroys our country and  someone's someone needs to rebuild  russian rocket strikes turned the house  from this  into this  with no hope of saving it the workers  clear the way for reconstruction there's  a spirit in the people that i can't find  anywhere else and  yeah it's it's  it's really touching on march 5th this  man his pregnant wife and his brother  were in the basement when rockets struck  somehow everyone survived  so many people have come together i'm  very grateful for that we'll try to  adopt rebuild and move forward to  victory  through pouring rain searing heat and  summer sun they work until the site is  cleared  these volunteers came here to listen to  some music have a good time and rebuild  their country brick by brick  when it's done anton pojak's  father-in-law who owns the home says  thank you  [Music]  the reward a free concert from ukrainian  electro folk band onuka whose singer  says she takes inspiration from the  youth of ukraine and its defiance in the  face of an ongoing war  i do feel it in their  eyes and i think that if we have such  powerful young generation so  impressive so  encouraged  i have no hesitation that ukraine won't  stand with this  then  they party as only their generation can  celebrating in a torrential downpour in  a cultural center gutted by shelling in  the region where the ukrainian army  drove out russian forces  john hendren al jazeera ivanivka  northern ukraine
 

